{0 httpkit-engine}

A single-owner HTTP/1 connection composes core values and strict codecs. It owns
no transport, clock, scheduler or callback. Use {!Httpkit_engine.server} or
{!Httpkit_engine.client}, then explicitly offer input, poll events, submit
commands, and acknowledge output prefixes.

A Backpressured command has no effects; retry it after progress. An Accepted
command must not be retried. The output slice is stable until acknowledgement.
There is one queued incoming event and a finite output byte budget. Metadata
retention is additionally bounded by the codec configuration.

A server admits one exchange at a time. Pipelined suffixes remain with the caller
until both directions complete and output is acknowledged. IDs belong to their
connection; equal numeric IDs from separate instances are not interchangeable.

A final server response before upload completion aborts the incoming body and
forces close. Explicit discard can instead drain the framing to completion.
Client early final responses stop an unfinished upload and prevent reuse. Neither
role silently retries a request. Expect waiting can be released by a received 100
or an explicit application policy decision.

Handoff validates CONNECT/upgrade context and waits for output acknowledgement.
The caller owns the unconsumed suffix and transport, which transfer together.
After Handoff the engine never closes that transport or emits another event.

A runtime adapter should put a deadline around graceful shutdown and abort if it
expires. Transport errors and cancellation must reach abort and wake native
waiters. Mutating one engine simultaneously from multiple domains is unsupported.

{!modules:Httpkit_engine}
